                %li
                  %a{:href => "#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences"} Worldline supersymmetry
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em spinning relativistic particle
            is a variant of the plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            which has an “internal degree of freedom” called
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            , a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            are
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
          %p
            As a 1-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %em spinning
            relativistic particle is like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            but with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+field"} fermion field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#WorldlineSupersymmetry Worldline supersymmetry
          %p
            We discuss how spinning particles automatically have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            in their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            %a{:href => "#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences"} references below
            for more and see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "string+theory+FAQ#DoesSTPredictSupersymmetry"} string theory FAQ – Does string theory predict supersymmetry?
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "-formulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polyakov+action"} Polyakov action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            on the 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            is actually 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            coupled to “worldline matter fields”, where the latter are the embedding fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \mathbb{R} \to X
            into the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
          %p
            It turns out that the generalization of this 1-dimensional gravity action to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            yields the action functional that describes ordinary Dirac
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors"} spinors
            – spinning particles like
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a{:href => "#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences"} references on worldline supersymmetry
            below.
          %p
            The worldline supersymmetry of fermions comes down to the fact that their
            = succeed "(-constraint)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            has a square root: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,D] = 2 D^2 = H\,,\;\;\; [H,H] = 0\;,\;\; [D,H] = 0
          %p
            characterize the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+super-Lie+algebra"} translation super-Lie algebra
          %p
            For appreciating this fact it is important to keep the ingredients of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            theory sorted out correctly: a supersymmetric theory on the worldline describes a spinning particle on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} spacetime
            coupled to some
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %em on target space
            induced by the worldline theory need not have any global supersymmetries (in particular no superpartners to the effective particle excitations). What will happen, though, is that the full target space theory induced under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            will be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theory on target space. Some of its solutions may have covariantly constant spinors (and hence global supersymmetry), but generically they will not, just like the generic solution to ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            does not have a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
          %h3#paritition_function Paritition function
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                as
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} indices
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genera"} genera
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientations
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
              \:
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                  %th
