                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duong+Phong"} Duong Phong
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Complex geometry and supergeometry
                %strong 2005
                (2007) 1-40 [
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0512197"}> arXiv:hep-th/0512197
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/current-developments-in-mathematics/Current-Developments-in-Mathematics-2005/Chapter/Complex-geometry-and-supergeometry/cdm/1223654523"} euclid.cdm/1223654523
          %h3#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences Worldline supersymmetry
          %p
            Among the early references that describe the observation that the supersymmetric extension of the worldline theory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            describes ordinary Dirac
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
          %ul
            %li
              %p F.A. Berezin and M.S. Marinov, Ann. Phys. (NY) 104 (1977) 336
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+Brink"}> Lars Brink
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Di+Vecchia"} Paolo Di Vecchia
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Howe"} Paul Howe
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Lagrangian formulation of the classical and quantum dynamics of spinning particles
                %strong 118
                (1977) 76 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(77)90364-9"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(77)90364-9
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p R. Casalbuoni, Phys. Lett. B62 (1976), 49
            %li
              %p A. Barducci, R. Casalbuoni and L. Lusanna, Nuov. Cim. 35A (1976), 377 Nucl. Phys. B124 (1977), 93; id. 521
          %p
            A survey of constructions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            supersymmetric action functional for spinning particles in various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background fields
            is given in
          %ul
            %li
              J. van Holten,
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 1
                Supergravity and Spinning Particles
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.iaea.org/inis/collection/NCLCollectionStore/_Public/28/002/28002527.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p An argument that for arbitrary backgrounds the spinning particle’s worldline action is supersymmetric is given in
          %ul
            %li
              Darius Gagné,
              %em Worldline Supersymmetry and Dimensional Reduction
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9604149"}> arXiv:hep-th/9604149
              )
          %p Textbook surveys of worline supersymmetry include
          %p the beginning of section 14.1.1 in
          %ul
            %li
              Tomás Ortín,
              %em Gravity and strings
              Cambridge University Press (2004)
          %p
            Derivation of the supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            action of the spinning particle in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            is around (3.6) of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Strassler"}> Matthew Strassler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Field Theory Without Feynman Diagrams: One-Loop Effective Actions
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9205205"} arXiv:hep-ph/9205205
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+P.+Sorokin"}> Dmitri P. Sorokin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+I.+Tkach"} Vladimir I. Tkach
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitrij+V.+Volkov"} Dmitrij V. Volkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleksandr+A.+Zheltukhin"} Aleksandr A. Zheltukhin
                = succeed "," do
                  %em From the Superparticle Siegel Symmetry to the Spinning Particle Proper Time Supersymmetry
                %strong 216
                (1989) 302-306 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(89)91119-2"}> doi:10.1016/0370-2693(89)91119-2
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Rachel H. Rietdijk,
                %em Spinning particles in general relativity
                Theoretical and Mathematical Physics, Vol. 98, No. 3 (1994)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Warren+Siegel"}> Warren Siegel
                , p. 194 of:
                %em Fields
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912205"}> arXiv:hep-th/9912205
                )
              %blockquote
                %p
                  There, first exercise IIIB1.3 gives the action expanded out in all components, and then the following exercise IIIB1.4 gives the reformulation in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superfield"} superfield
                  formalism which makes manifest that the action is invariant under worldline
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %li
              %p
                Roberto Casalbuoni, Joaquim Gomis, Kiyoshi Kamimura, Giorgio Longhi,
                %em Space-time Vector Supersymmetry and Massive Spinning Particle
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.mendeley.com/research/spacetime-vector-supersymmetry-and-massive-spinning-particle/"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                E. Boffo,
                %em Particles in the superworldline and BRST
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02041"}> arXiv:2207.02041
                \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
